## ABSTRACT
Bimetallic Fe/Ni-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with different Fe/Ni ratios were coated on $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ nanorods (NRs), and theperformancesoftheheterojunction photoanodes inphotoelectrochemicalwater spltingwereinvestigated.Thebandgaps and band positions of the MOFs could be modulated bychanging theratioof the Fe and Nicomponents.An ideal band alignment was achieved between the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NRs and bimetallic MOFs with an optimum ratio of $[\mathsf{F e}]/[\mathsf{N i}]=0.25/0.75$ ，which allowed efficient light absorption and charge separation. The coating of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 layer on the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NRs decreased the photocurrent density by $33\%$ . In comparison, $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL(Ni)-88 showed a modest improvement in photocurrent density $(0.85\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at $1.23\vee$ vs. a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)). When bimetallic $\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{M I L}(\mathsf{F e}_{0.25}\mathsf{N i}_{0.75})–88$ was coated on the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NRs, the photocurrent density reached $1.56\:\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which was an efficiency enhancement of 3.2 times. The mechanism underlying high photoelectrochemical performance was investigated.
## KEYWORDS
band-structure engineering, bimetallic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), solar water splitting, $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ nanorods, ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MIL-88
## Introduction
Hydrogen is a sustainable energy source that can address the global  energy crisis and  environmental polution  issues. Photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting is one of the most effective and clean methods for producing hydrogen fuel from water and sunlight [1] and achieving high energy yields. Among the various metal oxide photoanodes for PEC water splitting [2-4], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has received significant attention because it is stable over a wide $\mathrm{\pH}$ range, non-toxic, inexpensive, and stable against photocorrosion [5-7]. Despite such advantages, its wide bandgap $(3.0{-}3.2~\mathrm{eV})$ limits the photogeneration of charge carriers under visible light. Furthermore, a low electron mobility $(1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and short hole-diffusion length $(10{-}100~\mathrm{nm})$ ） can cause the rapid recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs, leading to a low solar-to-hydrogen efficiency [8,9]． Accordingly, various studies have explored enlarging the active surface area, controlling the bandgap by doping, funneling the charges by forming heterojunctions, and loading co-catalysts [10-14].
The rational design of a staggered band alignment such as a type II heterojunction or direct Z-scheme with another semiconductor is a prominent approach to enhance the PEC activity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [15,16]. A staggered band alignment allows the effective separation of photogenerated charge carriers, which suppresses carrier recombination [l7]. In heterojunction photoelectrodes, it is crucial to consider the energy bandgap and band positions of the two materials because the energy bandgap determines the light absorption rate, and the band positions determine the charge funneling path for the oxygen evolution reaction (OER） or hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). In this regard, various semiconductors have been integrated with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to form staggered band alignments, such as ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ [18-20], $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ [21,22], and CdS [23,24].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous crystalline structures composed of metal ions and organic ligands, which have shown various potential applications such as in photocatalysis [25], supercapacitors [26], gas separation [27-29], and gas sensors [30] because of their remarkably high surface area, controllable porosity, compositional diversity, and semiconducting properties [25]. In particular, semiconducting MOFs with enlarged reaction sites and various band structures are attractive platforms for designing photoanodes with staggered heterojunctions [3l]. Recently, heterojunctions between MOFs and metal oxide semiconductors such as TiO/Co-MOF [32], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125[33]，BiVO/Co-MOF[34,35]， and $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}/M I L\cdot101}$ [36] have been explored for PEC water splitting. Further tailoring the band structures of MOFs could provide diverse pathways to achieve high PEC performances, which could be accomplished by the functionalization of organic linkers [37, 38],use of mixed organic linkers [39], and design of bimetallic clusters [40]. In particular, the compositional variation of metal ions in MOFs can provide a new, promising, and straightforward tool to modulate the bandgap and band position. For instance, based on theoretical calculations, Aziz et al. suggested that the bandgap and band position in a porphyrin-based MOF (PMOF) can be tuned by changing the composition of two metal nodes [4o]. However, to the best of our knowledge, the enhancement of the PEC performance of MOF-based heterojunctions by establishing and optimizing a staggered band structure through the compositional control of bimetallic MOFs has never been confirmed experimentally.
In this work, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanorods (NRs) were solvothermally grown on FTO/glass substrates, and bimetallic $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–88$ was subsequently coated on the surface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ using an additional solvothermal process. Changing the Fe and Ni composition of the MOFs made it possible to optimize the bandgaps and band positions of  the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}$ heterojunction. In particular, $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}(F e_{0.25}N i_{0.75})-}$ 88 exhibited a remarkably enhanced photocurrent density of $1.56\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), while $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 showed a $33\%$ deteriorated photocurrent density $(0.33\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}})$ ）compared to bare $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ This was because $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}–M I L(F e_{0.25}N i_{0.75})–\xi}$ 8 formed an ideal Z-scheme band alignment (staggered), whereas $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL(Fe)-}}88$ formed a type I band alignment (straddling), both of which were confirmed by ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis)spectroscopyandultraviolet photoemission spectroscopy (UPS). This work suggested the potential for rationally designing MOF-oxide heterojunction photoanodes by engineering the band structures through the compositional control ofbimetallic MOFs.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Preparation of $\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ NRs on FTO/glass substrate
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs were hydrothermally grown on an FTO/glass substrate. In a typical synthesis, $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of titanium(IV) butoxide (Merck, $97\%$ ）wasdissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous HCl solution ( $\mathrm{i}5\mathrm{mL}$ $36\%$ $6\%\ \mathrm{HCl}+15\ \mathrm{mL}$ deionized water) and stirred under ambient conditions. The mixture was transferred to a Teflon-lined vessel, and an FTO/glass substrate was immersed in the solution with the FTO side facing down. The reactor was sealed and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ . The substrates were rinsed with deionized water. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs were annealed at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ to achieve a highly crystalline rutile phase.
2.2 Preparation of $\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{-MIL}(\mathbf{Fe}_{1-x}\mathbf{Ni}_{x})\mathbf{-}88/\mathbf{TiO}_{2}\mathbf{NRs}$
$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(FeNi)-88 was grown on the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs by a solvothermal reaction. First, 3 mmol of 2-aminoterephthalic acid was added to $15\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethylformamide (DMF, Merck, $99.8\%$ ， and $1.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $4.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Ni(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ were added to $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. The two solutions were homogenized by stirring under ambient conditions. The mixed solution was transferred to a Teflon-lined vessel and a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NR/FTO/glass substrate was immersed in it. The reactor was then kept at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}.$ The substrates were rinsed with methanol a few times and then dried in a vacuum oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe})–88/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL( $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.75}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.25})$ ${\cdot88}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5})–88/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs,and $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L(N i){-}88/T i O_{2}}$ NRs were synthesized by adjusting the $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ ratio, as follows: $6.0\mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was used to produce $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe})–88/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\ \mathrm{NRs},$ $4.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $1.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Ni(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ were used to produce $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.75}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.25}){-}88/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, 3.0 mmol of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $3.0\ \mathrm{\mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were used to produce $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}){-}88/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, and $6.0\ \mathrm{\mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Ni(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ was used to produce $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L(N i){-}88/T i O_{2}N R s}.$
## 3 Results and discussion
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs were first hydrothermally grown on an FTO/glass substrate, followed by the solvothermal reaction to grow bimetallic $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–88$ on the surface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs (Fig.l(a)). The length of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs was $\sim1.30~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and the diameter was
The photocurrent densities of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MIL(Fe)-88， $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL(Ni){-}88}}.$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–\delta$ 38 were measured in a $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ phosphate buffer solution (PBS) (Fig.2(a)). Compared with the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL(Fe_{0.25}N i_{0.75})-88}}$ exhibited a significantly higher photocurrent density $\left(1.56\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right.$ at $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE).This represents remarkably high photoelectrochemical performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based photoanodes (Table S2 in the ESM). The photocurrent densities of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–88$ series with different $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ ratios at $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE were compared to investigate their compositional effects on the PEC performance (Fig.S6 in the ESM). When pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 was covered on the surface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, the photocurrent density decreased by $33\%$ .However,when Ni was added to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88, the photocurrent density increased and peaked at an $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ ratio of 0.25:0.75.Accordingly, hereinafter, this discussion will focus on a comparison of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL(Fe)-88， $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL(Ni){-}88,}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–8$ 8 to understand the mechanism underlying the high PEC performance of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ bimetallic MOF. The photocurrent density was measured by applying a constant potential of $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE in the dark and under illumination to obtain the light on/off transient states of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs and ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ (Fig.2(b)). All the photoanodes showed a significant increase of the photocurrent density under the initial illumination, followed by a stable steady state. To examine the photoactivity and light absorption of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–88$ with different $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ ratios on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, the incident photon-to-current conversion eficiency (IPCE) spectra were analyzed from 300 to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ at $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ VS. RHE (Fig. 2(c)). The IPCE decreased across the entire range when $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 was grown on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, implying that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MIL(Fe)-88 impeded the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ from achieving effective light absorption and energy conversion. When $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L(N i){-}88}$ was coated on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, the IPCE increased at $330{-}340~\mathrm{nm}$ but decreased at $370{\mathrm{-}}400\ \mathrm{nm}$ ，suggesting that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Ni)-88 was more photoactive at a lower wavelength and inactive at a higher wavelength. On the other hand, $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}–M I L(F e_{0.25}N i_{0.75})–88}$ exhibited a remarkably high IPCE throughout the entire light absorption range. It showed the highest IPCE of $\sim80\%$ at $360\mathrm{nm},$ which was approximately 3.2 times that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs. The results indicated that UV-vis light (wavelengths of $300{\mathrm{-}}410\ \mathrm{nm}$ ）was effectively utilized for light absorption, charge separation, and charge transportation for PEC water splitting. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) study of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ MOFs was performed to investigate the charge carrier transportation (Fig.2(d)). The charge transfer resistance between the electrode and the electrolyte $(R_{\mathrm{{ct}}},\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{{cm}}^{2})$ was calculated by simulating and fitting the Nyquist plots (Table 1). The $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs increased when they were coated with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL(Fe)-88, implying that the charge transfer was hindered by the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 coating, which was in line with the decreased photocurrent density. Compared with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88, coating the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs with $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L(N i){-}}88$ produced a lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}},$ suggesting that the Ni provided a more facilitated conductive path than Fe. When bimetallic $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75}){-}8\8$ was coatedon the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value remarkably decreased to $662.2~\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{2};$ which was approximately $30\%$ and $70\%$ of the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ values that could be achieved with single metallic $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MIL(Ni)-88. This was consistent with the reported results that a bimetallic composition of Fe and Ni showed a higher conductivity than single metallic MOFs [49-51].
To reveal the dynamics of light absorption, charge separation, and charge transportation, UPS and UV-vis spectroscopy were carried out to investigate the band bending between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88 series. Figures 3(a) and 3(b) show the secondary electron emission (SEE) spectra of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ N $\mathrm{\AIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75}).$ -88, which reveal the energy from vacuum to the Fermi level (work function $\phi\mathrm{i}$ ) and the energy from the Fermi level to the valence band maximum (VBM). The work functions of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–88$ were 3.72 and $2.13~\mathrm{{\eV}};$ respectively; while the VBMs of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–$ 88were2.63 and $2.01\:\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ respectively. According to the UV-vis spectra shown in Fig. 3(c), the energy bandgaps of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–\$ 88were 3.0 and $2.56\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Based on the UPS and UV-vis spectroscopy data, the intrinsic band structures of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{2}–M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–88$ are shown in Fig.3(d). Because $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75}){-}88$ has a higher $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ value than $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ electrons were transferred from the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–88$ to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ until equilibrium was reached (Fig.S7 in the ESM) [52],which was consistent with the XPS results. At this moment, the upward band edge bending of the $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75}){-}8\xi$ 3 and downward band edge bending of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were induced by the internal electric field. The formation of an internal electric field promoted the recombination of photogenerated electrons in the conduction band $(E_{\mathrm{C}})$ of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and photogenerated holes in the valence band $(E_{\mathrm{V}})$ of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–$ 88. Consequently, a direct Z-scheme was established between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75}).$ 88, allowing the photogenerated charge carriers to be efficiently separated. It is worth noting that the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs partially coated with MOFs were in contact with both the MOFs and the electrolyte. Accordingly, through the Z-scheme heterojunction [15], a higher density of holes was separated, reached the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ electrolyte interface, and participated in the water oxidation reaction (Fig.3(e)), which explains the enhancement of the photocurrent density of the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}(F e_{0.25}N i_{0.75})-}\mathfrak{s}$ 88.Note that the photoinduced electrons at the $E_{\mathrm{C}}$ of MOF can reach the
FTO electrode when the external bias $(\sim\ 1.23\ \mathrm{~\eV})$ exists. Moreover, to observe the effect of MOF coverage on the PEC performance, the solvothermal reaction time was controlled up to $10\mathrm{~h~}$ to increase the MOF coverage on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface. Figures S8(a) and S8(b) in the ESM revealed that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})\mathrm{-}\Omega$ 88 was fully covered on the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ after $10\mathrm{~h~}$ solvothermal reaction. At $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE,the current density of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ N $\mathrm{\uIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})$ -88( $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ solvothermal) photoanode decreased to $0.95\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ when compared with $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL(Fe_{0.25}N i_{0.75})-8\delta}$ 8 (5 h solvothermal) photoanode (Fig.S8(c) in the ESM). The result implied that partial coverage of the MOF on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is crucial for obtaining the optimum PEC performance by establishing a direct Z-scheme. In addition, the band structures of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Ni)-88 were studied (Fig.S9 in the ESM). When the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 reached an equilibrium state, the $E_{\mathrm{C}}$ of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was located at a more positive level than that of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88,while the $E_{\mathrm{V}}$ of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was located at a more negative level than that of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MIL(Fe)-88. In this case, a type I band alignment was formed between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)- 88,where it was favorable for the photogenerated electrons in the $E_{\mathrm{C}}$ of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to migrate to the $E_{\mathrm{C}}$ of the -MIL(Fe)-88, while the photogenerated holes in the $E_{\mathrm{V}}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ tend to transfer to the $E_{\mathrm{V}}$ of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 (Fig. Si0(a) in the ESM). Consequently, the redox ability of photogenerated charges decreases after charge transportation along the junction [53],which could explain the deteriorated PEC performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88. Unlike $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88, a direct Z-scheme is formed between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MIL(Ni)-88 (Fig.S10(b) in the ESM), which supports the enhancement of PEC performance compared with pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ It is worth noting that the bandgap of bimetallic $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–8\xi$ 3 is smaller than that of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Ni)-88, leading to the more favorable adsorption of a broader range of visible light and consequent increase of current density. In this perspective, among the three types of band structures, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})-$ 88 demonstrated the ideal bandgap and band alignment, which provided efficient light absorption and favorable charge transportation path.
In addition to band alignment models, it is important to compare the three main factors in the PEC performance, the light absorption efficiency $(\eta_{\mathrm{abs}})$ ，charge separation efficiency $(\eta_{\mathrm{sep}}).$ and charge transfer efficiency $(\eta_{\mathrm{trans}})$ , in order to understand the charge flow dynamics during water splitting. These three factors are precisely expressed in the following equation, along with the water photo-oxidation current density [54-56]
$$
J_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}=J_{\mathrm{max}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{abs}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{sep}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{trans}}
$$
where $J_{\mathrm{max}}$ is the theoretical maximum photocurrent density under 1 sun illumination, and $\eta_{\mathrm{trans}}$ can be assumed to be $100\%$ when a hole scavenger (sulfite in this work) is introduced to the electrolyte to induce rapid oxidation kinetics [57]
$$
J_{\mathrm{sulfite}}=J_{\mathrm{max}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{abs}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{sep}}
$$
In addition, if it is assumed that no loss occurs in the charge separation and charge transportation process, then all the absorbed photons are fully converted to photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{{abs}}})$ , which can be simplified as follows
$$
J_{\mathrm{abs}}=J_{\mathrm{max}}\times\eta_{\mathrm{abs}}
$$
