## 4 Conclusions
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88(FeNi) photoanodes with different $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ ratios were prepared via a two-step solvothermal reaction for PEC water splitting. By changing the Fe and Ni composition, the bandgap and band positions of the MOFs could be tuned to establish a direct Z-scheme heterojunction with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The photocurrent densityof the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL(Fe)-88 photoanode was significantly lower than that of the pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ photoanode, which was attributed to the limitation of electron transport due to the formation of type-I band alignment. The $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL(Ni){-}88}}$ photoanode showed a direct Z-scheme band alignment, which produced a modest photocurrent density $(0.85\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE). Interestingly, coating the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NRs with bimetalic $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})–$ 88 produced the highest photocurrent density $(1.56\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE), which originated from the formation of the Z-scheme heterojunction and consequent suppression of charge recombination, enhanced light absorption efficiency due to the smaller bandgap of MIL $\mathrm{(Fe_{0.25}N i_{0.75})}$ -88, high charge carrier concentration of N $\mathrm{\varPiIL}(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.75})$ -88, and synergetic catalytic effect of Fe and Ni. This work suggests that the modulation of the energy-band structure, charge transport, and charge carrier concentration using bimetallic MOFs provides a promising strategy for designing a direct Z-scheme MOF-oxide heterojunction photoanode forenhancing the photoelectrochemical water splitting reaction.
